{% set local_problem %}
<p>Cullman's commitment to home maintenance means damaged fascia stands out — both visually and functionally. Peeling paint, water stains, and visible rot detract from otherwise well-maintained properties. More importantly, compromised fascia can't properly support gutters and allows water into roof structures.</p>

<p><a href="/recent-projects/">Older homes near downtown</a> face particular challenges. Original fascia boards may be approaching or exceeding their natural lifespan — 50, 60, even 80 years of Alabama humidity and occasional gutter problems have taken their toll. These homes deserve repair work respecting their character while restoring full function.</p>

<p>Even newer homes can develop fascia problems if gutters have been overflowing or leaking. Water damage accumulates over time, and rot can spread behind paint that looks fine on the surface.</p>

<p><strong>The uncomfortable truth:</strong> About 35-40% of gutter replacement jobs in Cullman need some fascia work. Not the whole house — usually isolated sections where gutters leaked or overflowed consistently. We'd rather tell you upfront than install new gutters on compromised wood that won't hold them.</p>
{% endset %}

{% set service_explanation %}
<p>We begin with thorough inspection — checking not just visible surfaces but probing for hidden damage behind paint and at seams. You'll know exactly what condition your fascia is in.</p>

<p><strong>For damaged sections:</strong> We carefully remove existing gutters, cut out rotted wood back to solid material, and install new fascia boards. We use quality materials appropriate to your home — standard primed lumber for most applications, matching profiles for historic homes where appearance matters.</p>

<p><strong>Material choice matters:</strong> Standard pine fascia lasts maybe 10-12 years in Alabama humidity. Composite or treated lumber lasts 20+ years. The upgrade costs 30-40% more but more than doubles the lifespan. For Cullman homes where quality matters, it's worth it.</p>

<p>New gutters are then installed on solid, properly prepared fascia. The result is a complete system: sound structure supporting quality gutters, designed to prevent the water problems causing damage in the first place.</p>

<p><strong>What we won't do:</strong> Slap new gutters on damaged fascia just to close the sale. Some gutter companies ignore fascia problems because they don't do carpentry. We'd rather lose a job than install gutters we know won't last.</p>
{% endset %}

{% set why_us %}
<p>We've been repairing fascia in Cullman County for over 20 years. We understand that for this community, quality matters — both the structural quality protecting your home and the visual quality maintaining its character.</p>

<p>We take time to do fascia work properly, using appropriate materials and careful craftsmanship. The repair should last as long as the home itself.</p>

<p><strong>Warning from experience:</strong> If someone offers to install new gutters and says your fascia "looks fine" without actually getting up there and pressing on it, get a second opinion. We've replaced plenty of gutters installed 2-3 years ago on damaged fascia — the original installer either didn't notice or didn't mention it. Now the homeowner pays twice.</p>
